 `Declare` is one declaration authority.
 
-`Declare` does not require one large inline function.
-
-Canonical examples must use thin orchestration functions and typed synchronous helpers for large command trees.
-
-Example:
-
-```go
-func declareRoot(command *cli.CommandBuilder[Root]) {
-    declared := declareRootInputs(command)
-
-    declareRootRules(command, declared)
-    declareRootCommands(command.Subcommands, declared)
-}
-```
+`Declare` does not require one large inline function, but lexical declaration is the default when it keeps semantic ownership, declaration order, and static topology clearer than extraction. A long declaration or orchestration function is legitimate when the visible composition is the architecture.
+
+Use typed synchronous helpers when they name a real reusable declaration pattern, semantic construction, integration operation, invariant, or ownership/lifetime boundary. Do not extract helpers merely because a declaration is long or because framework categories such as inputs, rules, flags, arguments, completion, or commands can be named.
+
+An application may use a `declareRoot` function when root application composition is independently meaningful. That boundary does not imply `declareRootInputs`, `declareRootRules`, `declareRootCommands`, or equivalent categorical helper layers. Static child staging should remain visible at the parent assembly boundary unless a stronger semantic boundary earns extraction.
 
